  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nShowers will wane tonight but clouds will largely remain in\nplace with overnight low temperatures mainly into the 60s. Fog\nis likely to develop, bringing reduced visibility. \n\nFog will lift Friday morning with partly cloudy skies allowing\nhigh temperatures to reach the lower to middle 80s south of the\nmountains. A cold front will cross from the north during the\nafternoon and early evening, which combined with daytime\ninstability and some shear will likely allow for showers and\nthunderstorms to develop. A few storms may contain gusty winds.\n\nCooler temperatures arrive behind the cold front with Friday\nnight lows into the 40s and 50s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBroad high pressure builds in from the west Saturday behind a \ndeparting cold front. A drier air mass will be in place with dew \npoints in the 40s and 50s with mostly sunny skies and highs in the \n70s to low 80s. High pressure sinks south Sunday as a trough swings \nacross Atlantic Canada. This trough will send a cold front into \nnorthern areas Sunday afternoon and evening bring a chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms. South of the mountains will remain dry \nwill mostly sunny skies. Highs will range from the 70s north the mid \n80s south with steady WSW winds allowing for mid 80s near the \ncoast.\n\nThe cold front will push south through the rest of the area Sunday \nnight and stall near southern New England Monday morning. Flow aloft \nwill trend towards zonal through mid week allowing the cold front \nbecome stationary near New England. The position of this stationary \nfront will effect temperatures with ensembles favoring the forecast \narea to remain on the cool side of the boundary. Latest \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance suggest the boundary will be far \nenough south to limit shower chances on Monday. Model spread in \nprecipitation increases Tuesday and Wednesday as waves of low \npressure track west to east along the boundary. The latest NBM \nbrings 40 to 60 percent PoPs during this time which seems \nreasonable with modest probabilities that portions of the \nforecast area will see an inch of QPF by the second half of next\nweek.",
